+		/* eth2 (2.5G) LEDs are driven by Linux, not by the vendor register
+		 * blob. The netdev trigger bound in /etc/config/system gives proper
+		 * link/activity behaviour here, and unlike the 10G port this PHY has
+		 * full LED support in the qca808x driver.
+		 *
+		 * Two kernel fixes are what make this work at all on 6.6:
+		 *  - of_phy_led() now honours "default-state", so the LED is forced
+		 *    off at probe instead of keeping its power-on value;
+		 *  - qca808x_led_parse_netdev() now maps TRIGGER_NETDEV_LINK onto the
+		 *    per-speed hardware bits, so "link" actually gates on carrier.
+		 *
+		 * active-low IS required here. The stock ETH_led_control.sh writes
+		 * 0x46 to the polarity register 0x901a (bit 6 =
+		 * QCA808X_LED_ACTIVE_HIGH) in its all_on(), which reads like the
+		 * board is active high - but on hardware that leaves both LEDs lit
+		 * whenever the LED logic says "off". With active-low (driver clears
+		 * bit 6) the LEDs track link and traffic correctly. Measured
+		 * behaviour wins over the inference from the vendor script.
+		 *
+		 * Polarity is global to the PHY, so both LEDs must agree - the
+		 * driver rejects a mismatch.
+		 *
+		 * /etc/init.d/eth-leds deliberately skips PHY 0x1c for the same
+		 * reason - two owners writing LED_CTRL would fight each other.
+		 */
 		leds {
 			#address-cells = <1>;
 			#size-cells = <0>;
